Situation:
The division is sustaining dispersed maneuver and support elements across a contested sector with constrained ground lines of communication. Key corridors include a mountain pass and a river valley route that are intermittently interdicted by enemy ambushes and IED activity. Weather and terrain limit convoy speeds and capacity; lateral routes are narrow and degrade under traffic. Airlift is prioritized for medical and high‑value items and cannot cover routine bulk resupply. Rear stocks are postured at multiple depots (e.g., Supply Depot Alpha, Depot Bravo, Depot Falcon, Depot Raven, Depot Summit), each holding differing levels of priority classes of supply. Forward units at several FOBs and COPs (e.g., Vanguard Infantry Battalion at FOB Iron, Archer Armored Company near Ridge, Pathfinder Recon Troop at OP Eagle, Guardian Field Artillery Battery at Fire Base Orion, Sentinel Air Defense at COP Horizon, Pioneer Engineers at FOB Raptor, and Nomad Support Company at FARP Theta) require timely resupply to maintain combat power.

Mission:
Deliver required classes of supply to all supported units during the current distribution cycle while minimizing total operational cost—combining transport costs per lane, vehicle deployment costs, fixed route security risk costs for any activated route, and penalties for any controlled shortfalls—without exceeding depot availability, vehicle capacity per convoy, or overall fleet availability.

Execution/Concept:
- Depot dispatch is capped by stock on hand by class and period; shipments from each depot for each supply type will not exceed available quantities.
- Unit demands must be satisfied; if full fulfillment is not feasible within acceptable risk/cost, controlled shortfalls are allowed and penalized to reflect operational impact.
- Vehicle loading is constrained by capacity; the volume footprint of each supply type determines how many vehicles are required per route.
- Vehicles may only use routes that are activated for the period. Activating any depot‑to‑unit route triggers a fixed security risk cost, regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched on that route. This represents escorts, route clearance, and overwatch commitments that must be made once a corridor is used.
- Total vehicles dispatched across all routes are bounded by the available fleet; vehicle deployments also incur a per‑vehicle cost.
- Transport costs vary by depot‑to‑unit lane. The plan should consolidate shipments onto fewer activated corridors when economical, balancing fixed route risk costs against per‑unit transport and vehicle costs, while preserving redundancy for critical nodes.
- Priority sourcing is from secure depots with available stocks and manageable transit risk; marginal routes should only be opened when the value of delivered supply outweighs the fixed risk and deployment costs.
- The optimization will select flows, vehicle allocations, and route activations that minimize total operational cost while meeting the commander’s sustainment intent and maintaining force protection.

